{"tool_name":"compleasm","contents":["bioschemas","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"compleasm","home":"https://github.com/huangnengCSU/compleasm","license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"Compleasm: a faster and more accurate reimplementation of BUSCO.\nIt provides measures for quantitative assessment of genome assembly, gene set, and transcriptome completeness based on evolutionarily informed expectations of gene content from near-universal single-copy orthologs.","addition_date":"2024-03-18T14:51:49.667412Z","last_update_date":"2025-06-30T15:30:41.266812Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["v.0.2.5"],"collections":["ONTeater"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:compleasm","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"neng@ds.dfci.harvard.edu","name":"Neng Huang","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7187-0749","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/huangnengCSU/compleasm/blob/0.2.6/README.md"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"cmd":"compleasm run -l \"$lineage\" -a assembly.fa -o output_prefix","input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence set (nucleic acid)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1234"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA-like","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2546"}]}],"note":"Runs compleasm using the BUSCO set corresponding to the lineage given.","operation":[{"term":"Sequence assembly validation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3180"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2955"}}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Compleasm","owner":"rlibouban","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btad595","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: Evaluating the gene completeness is critical to measuring the quality of a genome assembly. An incomplete assembly can lead to errors in gene predictions, annotation, and other downstream analyses. Benchmarking Universal Single-Copy Orthologs (BUSCO) is a widely used tool for assessing the completeness of genome assembly by testing the presence of a set of single-copy orthologs conserved across a wide range of taxa. However, BUSCO is slow particularly for large genome assemblies. It is cumbersome to apply BUSCO to a large number of assemblies. Results: Here, we present compleasm, an efficient tool for assessing the completeness of genome assemblies. Compleasm utilizes the miniprot protein-to-genome aligner and the conserved orthologous genes from BUSCO. It is 14 times faster than BUSCO for human assemblies and reports a more accurate completeness of 99.6% than BUSCO's 95.7%, which is in close agreement with the annotation completeness of 99.5% for T2T-CHM13.","authors":[{"name":"Huang N."},{"name":"Li H."}],"citationCount":95,"date":"2023-10-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"compleasm: a faster and more accurate reimplementation of BUSCO"}}],"topic":[{"term":"Genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0622"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"},{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0196"},{"term":"Transcriptomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3308"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"Compleasm","home":"https://bio.tools/compleasm","license":"Apache-2.0","version":"v.0.2.5","summary":"Compleasm: a faster and more accurate reimplementation of BUSCO.\nIt provides measures for quantitative assessment of genome assembly, gene set, and transcriptome completeness based on evolutionarily informed expectations of gene content from near-universal single-copy orthologs.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:compleasm:0.2.5/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"